import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import LegalNotice from '../../components/LegalNotice.astro';
---

<Base
  title="What the UK's Digital Regulators Said About Agentic AI, KYDE"
  description="The DRCF, which brings together the CMA, FCA, ICO and Ofcom, published a foresight paper on agentic AI. It is explicitly not policy, but it shows where four UK regulators see risk and why deploying organizations should keep their own record of what their agents do."
  datePublished="2026-05-02"
>
  <Navbar />

  <main>

    <!-- ══════════ HEADER ══════════ -->
    <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="bg-grid absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none"></div>
      <div class="relative max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 pt-36 pb-16 md:pt-48 md:pb-20 md:border-x md:border-line-0">

        <div class="mb-10">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
            <a href="/resources" class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-1 hover:text-ink-1 transition-colors">↑ Resources</a>
            <span class="text-ink-1">/</span>
            <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-1">Regulatory</span>
          </div>
          <div class="h-px w-full bg-line-0"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="max-w-3xl">
          <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold text-ink-0 tracking-tight leading-[1.03] mb-6">
            What the UK's Digital Regulators Said About Agentic AI
          </h1>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-xs font-mono text-ink-1">
            <span>Regulatory</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>5 min read</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <time datetime="2026-05-02">May 2026</time>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══════════ ARTICLE BODY ══════════ -->
    <section class="border-t border-line-0">
      <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 md:py-24 md:border-x md:border-line-0">
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,42rem)_280px] gap-6 md:gap-16 items-start">

          <!-- Body -->
          <article class="prose-kyde min-w-0">

            <p>
              On 31 March 2026, the Digital Regulation Cooperation Forum published a foresight paper titled <a href="https://www.drcf.org.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;The Future of Agentic AI&rdquo;</a>. The DRCF brings together four UK regulators: the Competition and Markets Authority, the Financial Conduct Authority, the Information Commissioner's Office and Ofcom. The paper sets out how they see the risks of AI agents across competition, financial services, data protection and online safety.
            </p>
            <p>
              The paper is explicit that it is not policy. It &ldquo;should not be taken as an indication of current or future policy by any of the member regulators.&rdquo; We take that at face value. It is still worth reading closely, because it shows what four regulators with overlapping remits will pay attention to as agents move from pilots into production.
            </p>

            <h2>Existing law already applies</h2>
            <p>
              The paper's central position is that agents do not sit outside existing UK law. Data protection, consumer protection, competition law, financial services rules and online safety duties continue to apply when an agent does the work instead of a person.
            </p>
            <p>
              The paper also notes that accountability can be fragmented, split across model providers, system providers and the organizations that deploy agents. Our practical reading for a deploying organization is a cautious one: don't assume that someone else in the chain carries the responsibility for what your agent does.
            </p>

            <h2>The themes worth noting</h2>
            <p>
              Commentary from law firms such as <a href="https://www.lewissilkin.com/insights/2026/04/09/the-drcfs-quiet-warning-to-businesses-on-agentic-ai-102momm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lewis Silkin</a> draws out several themes from the paper that matter for anyone deploying agents:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><span><strong>Data minimisation.</strong> Agents should work on a need-to-know basis, in line with the UK GDPR's data minimisation and data protection by design principles.</span></li>
              <li><span><strong>Consent and delegation.</strong> People should understand what they have consented to, or delegated, when an agent acts for them.</span></li>
              <li><span><strong>Meaningful human involvement.</strong> Where a decision has legal or similarly significant effects, human involvement has to be real, not nominal.</span></li>
              <li><span><strong>Algorithmic collusion.</strong> Pricing agents may converge on higher prices without anyone instructing them to, which creates competition risk.</span></li>
              <li><span><strong>Overlapping remits.</strong> A single deployment, such as a retail shopping assistant, can raise questions for all four regulators at once.</span></li>
            </ul>

            <h2>What the paper does not do</h2>
            <p>
              It does not create new obligations, and it does not announce enforcement plans. As Lewis Silkin observes, the next steps it proposes amount mostly to further research and continued dialogue. Anyone presenting the paper as new regulation is overstating it.
            </p>

            <h2>Why your own record matters</h2>
            <p>
              If responsibility can be split across several parties, the organization deploying an agent needs its own account of what that agent did: which data it accessed, which actions it took and on whose authority. Logs kept by a model provider are useful, but they are kept by the provider, on the provider's infrastructure. When a regulator asks questions, an organization that can produce its own record is in a stronger position than one that has to request it from a vendor.
            </p>

            <h2>Practical steps</h2>
            <p>
              <strong>Map each agent to the four remits.</strong> For every agent in production, note whether it touches personal data, consumers, pricing or competition, financial services or online content. That tells you which regulators may care about it.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Test your record.</strong> Pick an agent action from the last 90 days and try to reconstruct it from start to finish without asking a provider for help. Where that fails, you have found your gap.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Make human oversight real.</strong> For consequential decisions, check that a person can actually review and change the outcome, and that this happens in practice.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Review pricing agents.</strong> If agents set or recommend prices, look at how they behave in aggregate, not only one decision at a time.
            </p>
            <p>
              The paper is not policy. It is a clear look at what four UK regulators are watching, and that makes it a useful checklist.
            </p>

            <!-- KYDE callout -->
            <div class="not-prose mt-12 border border-line-0 bg-bg-1 p-8">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-2 mb-3">KYDE</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-1 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Kyde Score learns how each agent behaves from its own telemetry and alerts you when that changes. Kyde Control adds a gateway between your agents and your systems that holds unusual actions until a person releases them, and keeps a signed record of what each agent did, separate from your model providers.
              </p>
              <button
                onclick="openModal()"
                class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-white px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-mono text-cta-ink hover:bg-cta-hover transition-all"
              >
                Contact sales →
              </button>
            </div>

          </article>

          <!-- Sidebar -->
          <aside aria-label="Related" class="hidden md:block space-y-6 sticky top-28">
            <div class="border border-line-0 bg-bg-1 p-6">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-1 mb-4">Regulators covered</p>
              <div class="space-y-2">
                {["CMA, Competition & Markets Authority", "FCA, Financial Conduct Authority", "ICO, Information Commissioner's Office", "Ofcom"].map((r) => (
                  <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-1">{r}</p>
                ))}
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="border border-line-0 bg-bg-1 p-6">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-1 mb-4">Areas of law</p>
              <div class="space-y-2">
                {["UK GDPR", "Consumer law", "Competition law", "Online safety duties"].map((r) => (
                  <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-1">{r}</p>
                ))}
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="border border-line-0 bg-bg-1 p-6">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-1 mb-3">Related</p>
              <div class="space-y-4">
                <a href="/blog/eu-ai-act-omnibus-collapse" class="group flex items-start gap-2 text-xs text-ink-1 hover:text-ink-1 transition-colors leading-relaxed">
                  <span class="font-mono text-warn shrink-0 mt-0.5 group-hover:translate-x-0.5 transition-transform">→</span>
                  <span>The EU AI Act Is Coming. Regardless of What Brussels Decides in May.</span>
                </a>
                <a href="/blog/dora-ai-agents" class="group flex items-start gap-2 text-xs text-ink-1 hover:text-ink-1 transition-colors leading-relaxed">
                  <span class="font-mono text-warn shrink-0 mt-0.5 group-hover:translate-x-0.5 transition-transform">→</span>
                  <span>DORA and AI Agents: Why Your LLM Provider's Log Doesn't Satisfy Article 30</span>
                </a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </aside>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  <LegalNotice />
  </main>

  <Footer />
